      <h2>MouseMind: a mouse in a cage, simulated</h2>
      <p><strong>Status:</strong> prototype (Feb 2026). The source lives in a private repository, so there is no link or code here, just a short write-up.</p>

      <h3>What it is</h3>
      <p>MouseMind is a browser simulation of a mouse in a cage. Homeostatic drives (hunger, thirst, energy and sleep need) push its behaviour: it heads for food when it is hungry, water when it is thirsty, and sleep when it is tired. A day-night activity cycle shapes when it is active, and pause and speed controls let you watch it go about its day.</p>

      <h3>How it is built</h3>
      <p>The simulation engine is written in Go and streams its state to the browser over WebSockets. The frontend uses Svelte 5 for the UI and PixiJS 8 for rendering. The engine is fully decoupled from the renderer, so it also runs headless, which is how it gets tested.</p>
<pre>
+---------------------+   WebSocket   +----------------------+
|  Go engine          | ------------> |  Svelte 5 UI         |
|  (headless-capable) |     state     |  + PixiJS 8 renderer |
+---------------------+               +----------------------+
</pre>

      <h3>Approach</h3>
      <p>It was built spec-first: the specs were written before the code, and the implementation follows them.</p>

